WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.608 --> 00:00:08.400
MEHRAN NAZIR: Welcome
to Google Cloud Next.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.480
My name is Mehran Nazir.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.950
I am a product manager
with Google Cloud.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.750
And today I'll be
speaking to you

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.370
about how you can use BigQuery
to transform your organization

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.710
into a data driven one.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.160
I'm joined by Fabrizio
Blanco from Viant Technology.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.870
And he'll be going
into Viant's journey

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.535
to the cloud using BigQuery.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.820
So to kick things
off, an exercise.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:43.125
Does anyone recognize
what this number means?

00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:43.850
A hint.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:47.223
It's something that you
would find in a school

00:00:47.223 --> 00:00:48.140
anywhere in the world.

00:00:50.735 --> 00:00:51.235
Exactly.

00:00:51.235 --> 00:00:53.080
I heard someone in the
back saying that it's

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.670
the Dewey Decimal system.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.040
Exactly what it is.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.230
So let's break that
down bit by bit.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.120
Leading 0 is a classifier
for information.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.140
Second zero is for computing
information followed

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:13.030
by data processing and computer
science, networking, wide area

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:18.690
networks, internet, and
our final conclusion,

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.640
"Data Science on the
Google Cloud Platform,"

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.840
the title written by one of our
esteemed training team members.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.930
Now some of you if you've
walked into a library anytime

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.820
in the past 10, 15 years, you
might have recognized that.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.380
And for the most part,
it's a useful system.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:42.140
It's a little bit subject
to human error and manual

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.670
difficulties, but it is
very important and powerful

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.000
because it allows for people
like you and me to navigate

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.950
the treasure trove
of information

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.650
that a library is,
so that it doesn't

00:01:54.650 --> 00:02:00.100
belong to just subject domain
experts like librarians.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.860
But we live in a
different world today.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.880
We live in the
world of big data.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.039
Estimates put the
Library of Congress

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:13.090
at anywhere between 15
to 20 terabytes of data.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.640
For a big data
application, we can rack up

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.190
15 to 20 terabytes within days.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.830
In some applications
sometimes hours.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:24.490
And when you think about
a library that doubles

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.740
and triples in
sizes overnight, you

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.090
have a vast amount of problems.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.610
One, scaling infrastructure.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.570
It's not easy to find the
space to house all those books

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.450
and all that information.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.850
And related to that is finding
the specific people that

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.670
can manage that level of data.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.090
And to do both of those
things while controlling costs

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:50.060
is no trivial task.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.930
During my time on the
Google Cloud customer team,

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.960
I worked with many customers
with various big data

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.670
implementations.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:05.240
And what I realized was while
they would come to me very

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.840
often with a technology
problem, many times

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.730
it was really a change
management exercise

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.940
that was required.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.160
And during my time, I'd
see three main archetypes

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.350
of a data driven organization.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:25.550
One, you have the data driven
organization that is constantly

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.530
pulled in different directions.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.930
This particular
team is reactive.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.550
They don't necessarily have
the proper data infrastructure.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:40.340
And they tend to be really just
a supporter of the business

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.060
intelligence organization.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:49.570
The second type is one that is
fun but not very productive.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.150
This tends to not have a
very strong relationship

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.760
with the engineering
and product team, which

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.730
allows them to have very
limited product influence

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.830
and seems to be
typically unfocused.

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:05.470
The best kind of data
science organization

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.310
is one where data
science are treated

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:12.490
as thought leaders, ones
where data contributions are

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:15.300
recognized and celebrated.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.450
But most importantly, what I
want you to take away from this

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.209
is the fact that
data is democratized

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:23.640
and they are investing
in productivity.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.330
Importantly, when data is
accessible and universally

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.540
available to people
within the organization,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.390
it makes that organization
that much more tied

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.450
to data, which as we can
all agree in this room

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:41.650
is typically a company's
most important asset, which

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.895
transitioned to how do you do
data infrastructure with GCP.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:52.740
We know that your data comes
in all shapes and sizes.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.200
For your relational
needs, we have Cloud SQL.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.850
We have Cloud Spanner.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.180
For your nonrelational needs,
we have Cloud Bigtable,

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.820
Cloud Datastore for your
document database needs.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.730
But what I'll be focusing
on primarily today

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:08.820
is BigQuery, the crown jewel
of our data analytics platform.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.950
BigQuery is Google Cloud
platform's enterprise data

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:20.620
warehouse solution, fully
managed and serverless.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.680
If you're used to
managing backups

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.575
and working on disaster recovery
plans with your data warehouse,

00:05:27.575 --> 00:05:29.200
you no longer have
to worry about that,

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.390
because that is taken
care of under the hood.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:37.650
If you need to run
petabyte scale data and run

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.710
queries across
multiple tables, that

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.915
can now return
within mere seconds

00:05:42.915 --> 00:05:48.200
through a very convenient
and intuitive SQL interface.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.490
If you need to scale up
using CPU, RAM, and memory,

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.380
no longer do you
have to manage that,

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.590
That is all again managed
under the hood from the Google

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.460
engineering team.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.650
And more and more
what we're seeing

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.580
is our streaming
capabilities coming

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.910
to form in terms of
streaming dashboards.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.500
But as we know that technology
is really half the problem.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.510
The real problem is, again,
people and change management.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.710
And when we look at
enterprise users of data,

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:24.680
we see them in multiple
buckets, smallest level

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.440
being deep learning researchers,
which number in the tens

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.590
of thousands, machine
learning experts, which

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.152
is in the low
millions, engineers,

00:06:33.152 --> 00:06:34.610
which is another
order of magnitude

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:38.530
higher in the tens of millions,
but the largest sliver of that

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:42.440
are business users, business
intelligence folks, finance,

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.660
accounting, sales.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.360
All these people are the
biggest consumers of data

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.220
yet, if we don't have
data accessible to them,

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.810
that data is no longer an asset.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:57.120
So the question is, and
the question I pose to you

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:04.160
is how do you turn technology
from a barrier into an enabler.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.030
With that, I'll
give it to Fabrizio

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.260
to share Viant's story.

00:07:10.418 --> 00:07:12.210
FABRIZIO BLANCO: Thank
everyone for coming.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.180
Let me jump to the presentation.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:15.130
So what is Viant?

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:17.050
Viant is an advertising
technology company

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.030
that delivers the right
audiences across all inventory

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:21.610
formats and channels.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:24.760
And it's capable of measuring
its impact online and offline.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.340
The main piece of
technology we use to do that

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:30.603
is the DSP, which stands
for demand side platform.

00:07:30.603 --> 00:07:32.770
And since I'm going to be
talking about all the data

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:34.185
flows into the
DSP, I may as well

00:07:34.185 --> 00:07:36.310
give you a quick primer
about the real-time bidding

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:38.770
and the technology behind it.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.170
On this picture, you see
two sides, demand side

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:42.070
and the supply side.

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:45.130
The demand side is the
advertised technology ecosystem

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:47.890
the advertiser used to
often spend media dollar

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.810
or to buy inventory online.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:51.790
And the main piece of
technology used is a DSP.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:52.960
We have one of those.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.490
On the left side, you
have the supply side,

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:57.760
which is the technology
ecosystem a publisher used

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.002
to monetize their audiences
or sell inventory online.

00:08:01.002 --> 00:08:02.710
And the main piece of
technology they use

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:06.400
is the SSP, which stands
for supply side platform.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.940
The way advertisers buy
media online in real time

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:11.650
is via RDP protocol.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:15.520
And that's how the SSP and
DSP communicate to each other.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.650
To give an example, a
user comes to a publisher.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:19.913
A publisher can be an app,
a website, a video channel,

00:08:19.913 --> 00:08:21.580
audio channel, anything
you can imagine.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.913
The goal of the publisher is
to maximize the revenue out

00:08:23.913 --> 00:08:25.180
of this opportunity.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:27.490
The way they do that
is by auctioning off

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:30.440
this opportunity to multiple
buyers at the same time.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:31.989
And that's where
they use the SSP.

00:08:31.989 --> 00:08:35.710
The SSP is connected to
multiple DSP at the same time.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:37.600
DSP will just
leverage the data they

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.902
know about the user to decide if
they're willing to bid or not.

00:08:40.902 --> 00:08:43.360
If they're willing to bid, how
much they're willing to bid.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.458
And then if they
win the auction,

00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:46.750
that's called an ad impression.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:48.500
And the advertiser
gets to display the add

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.460
on the publisher, that happens
billions and billions of times

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.890
a day and generates
a lot of data.

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:56.410
Each single auction
is to be closed

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:57.790
within 15, 20 milliseconds.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:02.300
We're talking about very complex
ad stack and a lot of data.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:04.960
What does it look in terms
of data for buying DSP?

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.810
We process more than 100
billion events on a daily basis.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:11.380
Those events represent more
than 100 terabytes of data.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:14.110
On a second, we process
more than a million events.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:17.510
And each single event does
more than 700 data points.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:20.800
So when I talk about events,
I'm talking about these RTB

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.890
requests-- bid solicitation
as well as impression, clicks,

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:26.390
conversion, anything
you can do with ads.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:30.280
And how did we manage to
do that before BigQuery?

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:32.440
I'm talking about before 2014.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.720
So on the left side with
a custom ingest problem,

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:38.920
we ingest all this
relevant data into Hadoop.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.960
When it was entered
into Hadoop, we

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.480
say enter the data
warehouse ecosystem.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.290
But where there's a gray
box, you can see that--

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:47.620
the data warehouse ecosystem.

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.720
And the way the data
warehouse ecosystem

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.410
was managed by the
data warehouse team.

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:52.540
And there was a
handful of people

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.910
that were servicing the entire
organization for any data

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:56.030
needs.

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:57.970
And the way they were
processing the data

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:01.660
was through MapReduce, Pig,
and Hive on top of Hadoop.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:04.660
And the [INAUDIBLE] that emerged
typically out of this process

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:06.160
will be pushed
into Netezza, which

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.440
is a physical NPP appliance you
can query via SQL interface.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.360
And the way the business
would access the data

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.280
was through a business
intelligence tool

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.800
Tableau pointing to
these [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:19.603
Anything outside
those [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:19.603 --> 00:10:21.020
would need to go
through a process

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:23.050
of vetting polarization.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.038
So although this picture
looks pretty nice,

00:10:25.038 --> 00:10:26.830
it looks like everything
should be working,

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.420
the reality is it
failed to reflect

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:29.920
what was happening at the time.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.020
Everything should have
been on flame and melting.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:36.190
And the reason was we actually
had very hard time keeping up

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:37.510
with the scale of the business.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:40.090
Every day more and more
data coming in and more

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:41.410
and more requests coming in.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:43.957
And there are two sides
here on the scaling issue,

00:10:43.957 --> 00:10:45.790
one on the Hadoop side
and the Netezza side.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:48.400
The Hadoop side, although
relatively easily,

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:50.900
we could scale it buying
machine and storage.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:52.408
The business was unpredictable.

00:10:52.408 --> 00:10:54.700
It was very hard to predict
what was going on tomorrow,

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.360
next week, next year.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.210
And so we were
struggling and compromise

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:01.390
between CPU and storage
constantly and, of course,

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:02.950
spending time keeping
the costs down.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:05.710
On the Netezza side was
much, much harder to scale.

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:07.820
You just have to double
up on the cluster.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:10.330
And we're talking about millions
of dollars of investment.

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:12.580
Needless to say that that
data warehouse team, instead

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.890
of being an enabler
to the organization,

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:17.950
not because of their fault, was
actually a disabling factor.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.110
And the reason was
80% of their time

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:23.033
was spent maintaining
infrastructure, maintaining

00:11:23.033 --> 00:11:25.450
the cluster and making sure
the existing pipeline wouldn't

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:26.380
break.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:31.690
So long story short, late
2013, beginning in 2014,

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.760
we started looking
at alternatives.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.710
We did a bunch of
POCs and BigQuery

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:38.500
was the big winner
for us by far.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.480
We moved every single
piece of data into it.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:41.530
We never looked back.

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:43.390
It was the best
decision we ever made.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.260
The reason we picked
BigQuery is twofold.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:47.860
Number one, it is a
serverless infrastructure,

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:49.168
meaning it is a service to us.

00:11:49.168 --> 00:11:50.710
We don't need to
worry about scaling.

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.150
We don't need to
worry about tuning.

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.220
The second big reason is
you could query hundreds

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:57.190
of terabytes of data
within seconds and minutes

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.110
versus before it
would have taken

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:03.150
hours and days if the query
ever came back to start with.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:05.333
So how does the
architecture look like now

00:12:05.333 --> 00:12:06.250
that we have BigQuery?

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:09.100
Very much simplified
compared to before.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:10.840
Still we have a
custom Ingest problem

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:13.330
that pushed the raw
level data into BigQuery.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:16.660
The data warehouse ecosystem
right now it's only BigQuery.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:19.045
Every single piece of
data is inside BigQuery.

00:12:19.045 --> 00:12:20.920
The data warehouse team
will process the data

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:24.850
by leveraging all the API
available from BigQuery

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:27.430
to process and produce
the same [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.490
We used to produce before and
we're pushed into BigQuery.

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.060
And the way the business would
access data was through Tableau

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:36.010
pointed to BigQuery
at this time.

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:38.080
And then another
thing that we did,

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.650
we gave access to the user
using BigQuery native UI.

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:43.900
By giving access
to role level data

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:45.670
to a bunch of people
with SQL knowledge,

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:49.697
what happened is the
amount of requests that

00:12:49.697 --> 00:12:52.030
came through the data warehouse
team and the engineering

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.180
teams skyrocketed, because
all of these people

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:57.897
finally saw the richness
of the data that we had.

00:12:57.897 --> 00:12:59.230
And they never had access to it.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:00.250
All of a sudden they
started dreaming

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:02.230
about these data products,
all of this stuff

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:03.580
they wanted to build.

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:07.930
And long story short,
we remove completely

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:11.060
the bottleneck of our
infrastructure with BigQuery.

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:13.070
But then a new
bottleneck was created,

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:15.430
which was engineer
resources, because we

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:18.480
had this unbelievable
racecar, which was BigQuery

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:20.833
and only a few people could
drive it to its potential.

00:13:20.833 --> 00:13:22.750
And those were people
with engineering skills.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:26.110
That's when we decided to
build a couple of tools, Magnus

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:29.290
and Gliath, with the goal of
empowering as many people as

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:33.370
possible within the organization
to basically be a data

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:36.730
engineer like anybody else
with engineering skills

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:39.160
but without of course,
just with secret knowledge.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.170
They could do
everything on their own

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:43.510
without involving anybody else.

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.060
And that's where Magnus and
Goliath were born in 2015

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.862
and now available on Google
Marketplace as of 2018.

00:13:48.862 --> 00:13:50.320
I'm going to give
you a quick demo.

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:52.840
But before I do that,
the new architecture

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.530
looks very similar than before.

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.040
There are two main differences.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.170
Number one, Potens.io
tools on top of BigQuery

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:02.450
are now used for processing
and accessing the data.

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:05.140
Another big difference, a little
subtle here, you don't see it.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:07.030
But if you remember
previous two diagram,

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:09.430
there was this gray
box data warehouse.

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:10.755
That's gone.

00:14:10.755 --> 00:14:12.630
And the reason it's gone
is because two-fold,

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.160
the data warehouse engineers,
first of all, their job

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:16.390
description has changed.

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:20.140
And now our software engineer
building the Potens tool.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:23.170
This team is not servicing the
organization in terms of data

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:24.310
needs anymore.

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:27.670
Second is we actually fully
decentralized our data

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:28.630
warehouse.

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:31.538
And there is no central
point where requests come in.

00:14:31.538 --> 00:14:33.080
Everybody can do
everything they want

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:35.140
around data as long as
they have permission

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:36.830
on a specific data set.

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:40.990
And now I'm going to jump into
the demo, demo number one.

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.870
On this demo, right
now, I am an analyst.

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:47.890
I work for the
client services team.

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:50.470
And my clients, the DSP
clients, are the advertisers.

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:54.520
These advertisers have been
asking to get their data back

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:57.310
on a daily basis or whatever,
basically their impression

00:14:57.310 --> 00:14:57.820
logs back.

00:14:57.820 --> 00:14:59.750
And the reason is they
use multiple platforms

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:01.950
and want to aggregate
everything in one place.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.760
And for that, I
decided to use Magnus

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:06.190
because I'm very familiar
with Magnus, which

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:08.530
is our workflow automator.

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:11.340
And I want to make it
such a way that it's

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:14.380
generic such that I can use--

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:15.755
if a new client
comes in tomorrow

00:15:15.755 --> 00:15:17.297
and wants the same
thing, I shouldn't

00:15:17.297 --> 00:15:18.520
be able to change anything.

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:20.020
And the workflows adjust fine.

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.090
Before I jump into the
automation part of it,

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:25.515
I want to show you what
the data set looks like.

00:15:25.515 --> 00:15:26.890
And for that, I
am using Goliath,

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:29.470
which is our data
explorer, the tool that you

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:32.170
use to explore your data
sets, be able to query,

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:33.610
interact with data.

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.800
Specifically, I'm going to--

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:39.880
the data set I'm talking about
is ad impression data set.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:43.100
This is an actual
sample of actual data.

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:45.250
So we're talking about 28
terabytes of data right,

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:47.780
4.3 billion rows in total.

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:49.780
You can see quickly,
double-clicking,

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:51.640
you have a sense of
all the tables in here.

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:52.400
I can sort.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:55.720
I can see the size of each
specific table, the size.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.240
And then if I click
on it, I can see

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.910
that Goliath does
a very nice job

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:05.280
on grouping logically the
data by year, month, and day.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:06.910
I can mouse over a specific day.

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:09.290
I see exactly what the
size of this table.

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:12.240
If I click, I can see
the scheme of my table.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.850
And like I mentioned before,
we have this more than 700 data

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:17.040
points per single event.

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.310
An impression is an event.

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:23.090
And I can show you here
if I expand my list,

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:25.690
you can see that we
have all this in here.

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:29.070
Those are basically all
columns in our table.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:32.590
And for us was very important
to see that organized the way

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:35.705
we actually saw the data, which
is objects, within objects.

00:16:35.705 --> 00:16:37.330
Another thing that
was important for us

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:40.270
as well is to actually
see what the content looks

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:45.460
like for anyone that is trying
to work on queries on the data.

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:47.620
So we wanted to represent
the data as objects

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:51.080
as well, the moment you
preview or you query the data.

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:55.730
As you can see, I can go across
my columns and see the content

00:16:55.730 --> 00:17:00.280
and have quickly a glance
of what each single field

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.020
value could be like.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:03.640
And specifically,
now, since I'm going

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:08.560
to be talking about extracting
log from the impression tables,

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.369
one important field
I'm going to be needing

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:12.170
is the advertiser
ID, which is the one

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:15.748
I'm going to apply the
filter for that query.

00:17:15.748 --> 00:17:18.290
And since I said I want to make
this thing very generic, what

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:22.810
I'm doing is creating a table
called advertiser_to_extract.

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:25.400
And this advertiser_to_extract
has two columns.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:27.379
Number one is telling
me all the advertiser

00:17:27.379 --> 00:17:30.690
ID they need the data bank,
which is the first column.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.242
The second column is
telling me, well, definitely

00:17:33.242 --> 00:17:35.450
all advertisers, they don't
want the surrounding data

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:36.170
points that we have.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:37.340
They just want to
pick and choose

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.170
whatever fields they
want from the table.

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:41.360
And here, I'm describing
what they are.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.070
And now I'm going
to jump into Magnus,

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:45.410
which is how I'm going
to create this workflow

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:47.390
to automate this extraction.

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:52.550
So the first workflow is
called parallel_extract.

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:56.840
And before jumping into
specifics of the workflow,

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:59.150
you can see on the top
the name of the workflow,

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:00.710
the description, the owner.

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:03.770
I can set up a
lifetime of a workflow.

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:07.940
And more importantly, I can
schedule it as a cron, fixed,

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:10.730
or advanced as a cron job.

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:14.090
And then you can specify
a time zone as well

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:15.290
for this specific workflow.

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:18.000
On the right, you'll see
very important element here,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.330
which are what we
call parameters.

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:22.982
And parameters are two
kind, built-in and custom.

00:18:22.982 --> 00:18:25.190
Built-in is things that the
system generates for you,

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:29.660
Magnus generate for you, are
typically time and date-based.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:31.790
And custom is anything
you want to create.

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:33.552
Think about variable
and constant

00:18:33.552 --> 00:18:34.760
in your programming language.

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:38.450
They're used to make
your content dynamic

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:40.520
and create business
knowledge around it.

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:42.620
I'm going to show you how
I use these parameters.

00:18:42.620 --> 00:18:45.290
So the first task I'm using
right now-- by the way,

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:47.000
you can create several task.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.590
And you put them sequentially.

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:51.320
And that describes the workflow.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:53.060
My first task is a loop task.

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:56.060
As a loop task, it
requires an input table.

00:18:56.060 --> 00:19:00.060
This table, I'm referencing
it via this variable.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:04.820
And this variable is the
table I just show you before,

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:06.440
which was a two
Potens, which is one

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:10.220
that describes the advertiser
at the end and field.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.680
And then it needs
a parameter, which

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:15.740
is called, in this
case, advertiser,

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:19.300
which is going to iterate
over and pull a row per table.

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:22.040
Every time I take this table,
I'm going to pull a row.

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:26.410
And this variable
is of type record.

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:28.130
And you can see here
the way it looks.

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:31.640
It's basically the scheme
of the table, advertiserid,

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:33.820
and the string
which is the field.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:35.950
Another thing
inside this loop, I

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:38.810
have only one task,
which is a BigQuery task.

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:40.883
And what this thing does
is just running a query.

00:19:40.883 --> 00:19:42.800
But as you can see, there
are dynamic elements

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:46.130
inside the query, which
is the table itself I'm

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:47.750
going to extract the data from.

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:49.760
I'm using this variable.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:54.050
Then the filter itself,
which is the first element

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.840
of the record, which
represent the advertiserid.

00:19:56.840 --> 00:20:00.020
And this is the
fields that I need out

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:01.160
of this specific query.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:05.450
And then another
thing I wanted to do

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:08.405
is make the output
of the table generic.

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:14.140
I want to post fix the
tables with the advertiserid

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:16.550
and the data I'm
processing with.

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:19.120
Another thing that was very
important for my client

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:22.860
is to get the data
as soon as possible.

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:24.620
So what I wanted to do is--

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:27.530
typically, you have a workflow
that runs sequentially.

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.272
And sequentially
because a specific task

00:20:30.272 --> 00:20:31.480
depends on the previous task.

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.513
Well, in this specific
case, I really

00:20:33.513 --> 00:20:35.930
don't need things sequential
because every single query is

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:37.013
independent of each other.

00:20:37.013 --> 00:20:39.530
Through macros, I can
check, simply say,

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:43.730
run this asynchronously and make
them all run at the same time

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:45.448
to maximize time.

00:20:45.448 --> 00:20:46.990
And then at the end
of the loop task,

00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:49.040
it just simply wait for
all of them to finish.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:50.330
I can set up a time out.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:53.310
I can get a notification in
case there was any error.

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:57.260
So now I'm going to run this
query, this workflow by hand.

00:20:57.260 --> 00:21:00.770
And the output of these
tables will show up

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:02.270
inside this data set.

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:04.760
Right now, it's still be empty.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:06.810
And while this
workflow is running,

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:11.190
I want to show you how you
actually check what's going on.

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:13.400
You look at the workflow
setting, specifically

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:15.770
for this workflow, you can
see it is still running.

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:18.220
The previous execution
actually finished,

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.030
So I'm clicking on this one.

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:23.420
And you can see that the 10
iterations got submitted.

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:25.940
The loop task finished
in 14 seconds.

00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:27.860
All 10 queries got submitted.

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:29.810
I can drill down on
any query and see

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:31.670
what was the query
that got submitted.

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:34.220
You see the macros
got expanded for me.

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:38.130
You can see as well how my state
is scanning the specific query.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:40.273
And you can check every
single one of them.

00:21:40.273 --> 00:21:42.440
And then at the end, you
see there is the wait task.

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:44.570
It's waiting for all
of them to finish.

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:46.130
And let's see if
now it's finished.

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:47.047
It's not finished yet.

00:21:47.047 --> 00:21:51.068
But the tables start
showing up here.

00:21:51.068 --> 00:21:52.860
And actually, all of
them are finished now.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:55.530
You can see the 10
advertiser are all done.

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:58.260
And if I click on here to see
if it did what it was supposed

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:01.370
to do, well, for
advertiser 3312,

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:03.570
you need an xid time
stamp request URL.

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:06.270
And [INAUDIBLE] all
of them work properly.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:08.040
One thing I want to
point out, OK, I'm

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:09.620
done with this specific demo.

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:12.300
But one thing is
it was relatively

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:13.530
simple to build with Magnus.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:15.780
Just three steps,
really, three tasks.

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:18.630
But if I had to do
this outside of Magnus,

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:20.610
I would have needed
to ask an engineer

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.880
to build this software around
it, create a bunch of threads,

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:26.430
push a bunch of jobs
out, wait for them

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:28.860
to finish in another
thread, and then

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:30.780
ask system engineers
to have a machine

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:33.270
to run this thing
on a cron job, worry

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:34.500
about all this monitoring.

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:36.532
All of that, it is
completely gone with Magnus.

00:22:36.532 --> 00:22:38.240
We don't need to worry
about any of that.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:39.810
It's just managed
transparently, it's

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:43.110
a service on top of a
service, which is BigQuery.

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:46.170
So next example,
next example is going

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:48.430
to be a little more
sophisticated example.

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:51.270
In this case, I want to showcase
how you can connect Magnus

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:54.690
with other system
via an API task

00:22:54.690 --> 00:22:58.210
because Magnus can be initiated
via manually or through a cron

00:22:58.210 --> 00:22:58.710
job.

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:00.870
It can be called the API.

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:02.970
We can trigger an
execution on Magnus

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:05.530
with a bunch of GCP events.

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:08.730
But how do you connect
Magnus with external system,

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:12.400
let's say with other GCP
API or your internal API?

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:13.920
Well, we have an API task.

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:18.780
Of course, with the API task the
world of opportunity opens up.

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:22.300
And specifically, now,
I am a product manager.

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:24.360
I work for the DSP.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:29.550
And I have-- my advertisers
have been telling me lately

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:32.070
that it takes too long
for my images creatives

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:32.742
to be approved.

00:23:32.742 --> 00:23:34.200
And the reason it
takes too long is

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:39.000
because we have humans
involved that check physically

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:42.652
if the image violates
our content policy.

00:23:42.652 --> 00:23:44.610
And as a product manager
I have the idea, well,

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:46.470
I know how to improve this.

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:49.860
I would like to have my
engineers build this thing.

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:51.210
But engineers are always busy.

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:52.980
So I decided to
take it on my own.

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:54.855
And the reason I decided
to take it on my own

00:23:54.855 --> 00:23:57.270
because I'm familiar
with Magnus.

00:23:57.270 --> 00:24:01.440
And also I see that Magnus
has this API task and a bunch

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.960
of templates that available to
make it very easy to connect

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.480
to any API from
the GCP ecosystem,

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:09.900
including Dataflow, Pub/Sub,
Google Cloud Storage, machine

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:12.160
learning-- which I'm going
to show you right now

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:14.173
et cetera-- so it's
very, very easy.

00:24:14.173 --> 00:24:16.590
And you don't need to worry
about rejection authentication

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:20.940
because the token gets passed
automatically by Magnus.

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:23.910
So at that point, I
decide, OK, it's cool.

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:24.580
I can do it.

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:28.260
I did some research
on Google Vision API.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.360
And I see they have
a specific API which

00:24:30.360 --> 00:24:33.510
is exactly what I need,
which is annotation

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:37.620
API with a safe search
detection type of annotation

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:40.290
that takes in input an
image, and gives me back

00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:43.530
if the image contains adult
content, violent content,

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:44.910
racist content, medical content.

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.450
It's exactly what I need.

00:24:46.450 --> 00:24:50.190
So what I'm going to do is just
build this Magnus workflow,

00:24:50.190 --> 00:24:54.080
which is
annotate_new_creatives_demo.

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:57.500
Since it's a little more complex
compared to the previous one,

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:59.480
I'm going to give you
a view because one

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:01.435
of the task, for
instance, I am using here

00:25:01.435 --> 00:25:03.050
is calling a workflow.

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:06.860
So you can modularize your code
and create a bunch of tasks

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:10.130
within a workflow and then
make other people use it.

00:25:10.130 --> 00:25:13.100
This case, to show
you the entire view

00:25:13.100 --> 00:25:16.325
of what's going on here,
I'm showing you the preview.

00:25:16.325 --> 00:25:19.430
At 1,000 feet level, these
are the name of the task.

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:22.040
You can drill in on
each one of them.

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.470
So what this process
will do is just check

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:26.090
if on the creative
table there are

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:28.520
any new creatives being pushed.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:30.050
If there are any
new creative, I'm

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:32.690
going to call the
classify_ads workflow.

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:33.620
If not, I'm done.

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:35.320
There is no new
creative to process.

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:36.590
So I've finished.

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:38.360
The classify_ads
workflow, what it does

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:42.630
is loops in batch and batches
the creative in input just

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:44.630
to make sure I don't want
to call one at a time.

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:47.520
I just want to batch as
many as the API allows me.

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:53.000
And then creates the payload
with this generate_payload.

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.790
In basically, the API
requires a JSON payload,

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:58.450
I'm using BigQuery to
create this payload,

00:25:58.450 --> 00:26:02.780
pass it to the API task, and
then take the result back.

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:07.190
And on the calling
workflow, I'm checking here

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:09.617
if there was any creative
that got flagged.

00:26:09.617 --> 00:26:11.450
If there were any
creative that got flagged,

00:26:11.450 --> 00:26:13.400
I send an email out.

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:15.950
So I'm going to execute this.

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:18.020
And I should be receiving
an email shortly.

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:19.460
But while this
thing is executing,

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:23.520
I'm going to give you a little
more insight into the workflow.

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:28.030
So the first workflow is
what I just described before.

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:31.070
I'm checking if there are
any new creatives to process.

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:35.270
And the way I'm doing this
is by leveraging the fact

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:38.370
that parameters are actually
persisted for Magnus.

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:41.360
So I'm using the result
of the previous execution

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:44.720
to check what was my last
ID that got processed.

00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:48.900
And then I'm just using it
as a parameter on this query,

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:52.070
take every new creative
since the last creative ID,

00:26:52.070 --> 00:26:53.900
take the output and
put it on this table.

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:58.470
This table is described
here, creatives_input.

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:00.510
Then what I do is count
the number of rows

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:02.500
out of this query,
which is specifically

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:04.800
count the rows on this
table and assign it

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:07.650
to this variable,
which is new_rows.

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:10.140
And then if this is
the if/then condition,

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:12.870
I check if there
are any new rows.

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:15.380
If it's greater then zero pretty
much, go to the next task,

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:18.120
otherwise finish because
they have nothing else to do.

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.730
The next-- by the way, this
flashing means workflow

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:22.080
finished.

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:23.640
I'll show you the email soon.

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:25.230
The next workflow,
which actually

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:26.790
does the job of
classifying the ads,

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:30.510
is this classify_ads workflow.

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:33.030
Workflow take inputs
and those basically

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:35.460
are the parameters for
the workflow, which

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.750
is the destination table
where this annotation need

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:39.270
to be stored.

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:41.880
The source table,
the input to my API

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:43.680
without all this
creative, I need to check.

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.885
The batch size as well
as the type of annotation

00:27:45.885 --> 00:27:49.870
that I need to call the
Google Vision API with.

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:54.410
And then I'm basically storing
my ID for the next iteration

00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:57.150
so I remember where
to start next time.

00:27:57.150 --> 00:27:59.790
And here I'm checking
if any creative

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:01.990
got flagged with a SQL query.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:03.110
Let me go back here.

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:04.690
And I should have
received the email.

00:28:09.150 --> 00:28:11.690
OK, there's a new email
that just got sent.

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:15.200
As you can see, it's
basically an HTML table

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:17.480
with the first HDID
of the creatives.

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.850
The second is the image
that got annotated.

00:28:19.850 --> 00:28:22.760
And as you can see
here, these three images

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:27.190
have medical annotation
type of-- there's

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:29.900
a possibility there is medical
content inside this image.

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:31.700
And the same thing
for all three of them.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:33.950
So it worked, meaning
it's able to detect

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:36.350
there were three creative,
possible medical content.

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:38.070
By the way, for the
purpose of the demo,

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:41.678
I just disable the check
across adult content

00:28:41.678 --> 00:28:44.220
and that's just to avoid showing
something on the screen that

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:45.590
was not appropriate.

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:50.450
As you can see here, you see
the lines I commented out?

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:52.490
And this is just
running the query.

00:28:52.490 --> 00:28:54.950
It's generating my
little HTML table

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:58.010
that I'm sending via email here.

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:02.200
And here, the email task, I'm
simply say, OK, format as HTML.

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:05.640
This is the editor of the table.

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:07.670
And these are the
three rows you just

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:12.415
saw with this variable, which
is being computed by this query.

00:29:12.415 --> 00:29:14.540
And now I'm going to show
you by clicking here what

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:17.270
exactly those query returned.

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:20.150
If I run this query,
you'll see that the three

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:21.440
rows were this ones.

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:24.510
Means basically, I'm showing
an image in the content.

00:29:24.510 --> 00:29:27.160
And as you can see,
this query shouldn't

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:30.290
run because that is
variables in here.

00:29:30.290 --> 00:29:31.100
But guess what?

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:33.380
When I click from Magnus
and jump into Goliath,

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:36.380
all parameters that I need to
be able to execute the query

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:38.520
are being brought over.

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:41.960
And I can show you the used
ones for the specific query

00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:42.920
were those ones.

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:45.710
So I am able to debug
specific query the way

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:48.530
they are without changing them.

00:29:48.530 --> 00:29:50.450
So say that, I want
to show you how

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:53.840
I generate the payload from the
classify_ads internal workflow.

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:58.460
This is the execution
of the classify_ads.

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:00.680
So I was batching the creative.

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:03.590
Basically, I'm using the limit
and the offset of the creative

00:30:03.590 --> 00:30:05.780
to batch through a
bunch of creatives.

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:09.140
And then, I'm
basically generating

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:11.540
the payload via a query.

00:30:11.540 --> 00:30:13.610
You'll see what this query is.

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:14.540
This query is a--

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:16.550
I mean, it looks complicated,
but it's basically

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:21.890
I'm generating a JSON object
out of three rows on a table.

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:23.930
And I can cut and paste
this so you'll see what

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:25.646
the output of this query is.

00:30:34.394 --> 00:30:36.030
This is the output
of this table.

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:39.030
It's basically a payload
for my API, which is a JSON

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:42.630
representation of the images.

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:47.130
And then if I look here, the
next task that got executed

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:49.820
is the call of the
annotation API.

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:52.230
What I passed in
input is this JSON

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:55.200
that I prepare with
the previous queries.

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:56.820
And that's the output back.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:30:59.160
So for each query, it's
telling me the probability

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:02.220
of each type of content.

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:05.680
And then the next task, I'm
using JavaScript task to--

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:11.010
it's out of sync, sorry--
to populate the table itself

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:12.940
and take the result back.

00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:16.500
So this, again, is to showcase
a little more complex use

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:18.840
case to be able to
connect with several APIs.

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:21.800
But you can have
microservices internal to you.

00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:23.730
And you can do a similar thing.

00:31:23.730 --> 00:31:26.550
One thing I want to
show you is now--

00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:30.230
can you bring it back to
the presentation please?

00:31:30.230 --> 00:31:31.776
To the slides?

00:31:31.776 --> 00:31:32.276
Yes.

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:36.800
How do we use--

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:39.890
what are the stats of
Potens within Viant?

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:41.940
Viant is not the only customer.

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:43.120
Viant built Potens.

00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:45.490
But we have multiple
customers of Potens.

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:48.130
Those are stats only
within Viant itself.

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:51.770
Field today, we have about
360 user using the platform.

00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:54.000
And by the way, we don't
have that many engineers.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:56.630
It's all BI team,
research analytic team.

00:31:56.630 --> 00:32:01.120
They're product people, anyone
you can imagine using the tool.

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:04.150
Those people generated
5,000 workflow.

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:07.390
Total number of tasks across
all workflow is 180,000.

00:32:07.390 --> 00:32:10.390
You can see somebody went
crazy and built a workflow

00:32:10.390 --> 00:32:12.580
with 3,200 tasks in it.

00:32:12.580 --> 00:32:15.010
So it must have been
something complex.

00:32:15.010 --> 00:32:16.480
But the average is about 36.

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:19.420
Probably the median is
much smaller than that.

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:23.500
And then this is the current
month, the way Potens is used.

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:25.620
130 users use Magnus.

00:32:25.620 --> 00:32:30.280
Those 130 user generated
600,000 workflow executions.

00:32:30.280 --> 00:32:34.310
These workflow execution
generate 2 queries in BigQuery.

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:37.120
And these 2 million query
process 16 petabytes of data.

00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:38.940
That's on a normal month.

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:41.360
It was last month.

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:44.440
If you're wondering how
tasks are used within Magnus,

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:48.460
well, BigQuery, to no
surprise, is the favorite task

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:51.112
because that's the
workhorse where

00:32:51.112 --> 00:32:52.320
all the things are happening.

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:54.910
The big data crunching is
happening through BigQuery.

00:32:54.910 --> 00:32:57.220
The next most popular
task is GOTO task.

00:32:57.220 --> 00:33:00.400
It does make sense because you
do business logic through that,

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.830
if/then/else conditions.

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:07.700
The workflow tasks is
very much used as well.

00:33:07.700 --> 00:33:11.350
That's because you want a
modularize your workflows.

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:13.480
You can have people
build something

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:16.160
a little more complex like, for
instance, this classify_ads.

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:18.160
And you, as a user,
you can leverage it

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:20.170
by calling it as a library.

00:33:20.170 --> 00:33:23.410
Then the API task is just to
connect with other systems.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:25.360
And then the script
has to manipulate data

00:33:25.360 --> 00:33:27.280
in the email, although
is a small percent.

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:28.310
But it's very much used.

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:30.685
But, of course, you have one
email probably per workflow.

00:33:30.685 --> 00:33:33.880
So that's why it's only 1%.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:36.160
And that's it for
the presentation.

